     1	# Chapter 14 — Nineteen
     2	
     3	He managed twenty-one lying on his left side on a plank table with the sun on the ceiling, and told her from the beginning, because the beginning was the only place he had ever found to start it.
     4	
     5	Fourteen. A certification office in Denvash, a sigil that lit and went dark for eleven seconds and came back with a word on it nobody living had seen. A year in an Unranked District with a notebook. A training floor, and a girl with a Wind Path who did not know she was teaching, and a sidestep his body made before his foot decided to, and a page in the log with a question mark on it. He gave her the first notice, and the format of it, and that he had never once received the other kind. He gave her the count — nine before this morning — and the sources, by Path and not by name, because the names were the circle's and she had not been in the circle; and then, because she was a healer and had had her hands on three of them, he gave her three names anyway. Brom's. Karis's. Lira's. He told her what the conditions were as he had them, and that the model said real and not fight, and that until an hour ago nobody had ever tested that sentence.
     6	
     7	She did not interrupt. She stood with her hands flat on the table's edge either side of his shoulder, leaning on them, and looked at the wall where the sun had been, and listened the way she had read: without a verdict attached, going along it.
     8	
     9	"On your table," he said, "the conditions came together. I felt them come together, and I felt it start, and I could have stopped it, and I didn't. I reached. On purpose. The notice came while your hands were still on me." He said the notice to her, line by line: the exact wording that had stood on the wall. "*Clinical.* It's never said that before. It's never said anything that wasn't a fight, or the next thing to one."
    10	
    11	"Tide-adjacent," Oryn said. It was the first thing she had said in twenty-one minutes, and it was a word she was trying for weight.
    12	
    13	"Yes," he said.
    14	
    15	"Partial."
    16	
    17	"They're all partial. They're all *adjacent*. I've never had the whole of anything." He kept his eyes on the ceiling; the stillness was easier with the eyes fixed. "There's a page. I wrote it last night, before, in the loft, so that I'd have to pay what's on it and not what I felt like paying after. It's in the satchel. It's yours to read. It says what I knew and what I didn't and why I didn't tell you before, and the last reason isn't a good one, and it's on the page with the good ones."
    18	
    19	"Get it."
    20	
    21	He got up off the table.
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	He went up the loft ladder and came down it with the leather book, and she had not moved from where she stood; she had turned the stool to face the window and sat down on it, and she took the book from him without looking at him and opened it to the pencil.
    26	
    27	He stood, and did not know where to put his hands, and put them at his sides, which was where she kept hers.
    28	
    29	She read it as she read Karis's turned notebook: once, the whole of it, and then the parts that had a count in them a second time. *Sustained. Stakes. Earnest.* Her lips moved on the conditions. They did not move on the price. She read *she can't consent to what she can't know about* and stopped there for the length of one of her breaths, and read on, and read *the real one is that I don't know what she'd say*, and read on past that as if it were a wound she had already seen the extent of. Then *I want it. Write that too.* Then the last line. Then she closed the book on the pencil and held it shut on her knee with both hands, as she had held the arm.
    30	
    31	"So I couldn't read you because there was nothing in you shaped like a Path for the reading to find."
    32	
    33	"I don't know. Maybe."
    34	
    35	"I don't know either. I'm a healer, not a theorist." She was looking at the book's cover and not at him. "What I know is you've now got a piece of the thing I read with."
    36	
    37	"Yes."
    38	
    39	The pause was a healer's. He had watched her take it over Lira's leg on the mountain and over Brom's forearm at the stone: the count going to its end, and the finding not said until it had.
    40	
    41	"Then use it on yourself first. Before anyone." She looked up. "I want to know what you find, and I want to be the one you tell."
    42	
    43	He had been asked for a great many things by a great many people about what he was — a demonstration, a transcript, a baseline, a sentence with the hole in the right place — and he stood in a steading's back room and catalogued that nobody had ever attached a condition to it before. Not a price; the Compact priced it. A condition: a thing he would have to do first, and a person he would have to tell, both hers to set, and both right. He had a page on her knee that said what he had chosen for her, and she had read it, and this was her answer, and it was the only one in the log that had ever come back with terms.
    44	
    45	"Yes," he said. "First. And you."
    46	
    47	"Good." Oryn gave him the book. "Now lie down again. Not for me. You're the colour of the wall and you've been standing on that calf for a quarter of an hour, and I've just been told I'm a patient's source, and I'd like a minute to sit with the second thing before I go and eat."
    48	
    49	He lay down. She sat with it. Neither of them said anything for a while, and then she got up and opened the door, and the steading's woman was on the other side of it with a bowl, because the woman had known how long the long one took.
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	It came on at dusk, and it came in a register the log had no words for, so he built them on the first night and spent two days finding out whether they held.
    54	
    55	The cascade was the cascade. He had banked for it since Ostrand, and the discipline held as it had held eight times: the circle told on the loft ladder before dark, in four sentences; nothing scheduled; water and the last of the bread within reach of the grain he lay on; and Brom on the ladder's head with the lantern turned down to a thread, because it was his turn, and the rota had never once been discussed.
    56	
    57	What arrived was not a pull. He had had appetites before — motion, contact, the air, fixity — and every one of them had wanted something from him, and a want could be named and scheduled round and, in the end, put in an idle state and told to be quiet. This did not want. It *listened*.
    58	
    59	He lay on his back on the sacks in the dark and heard himself.
    60	
