   138	
   139	The birthday fell on the third day, and Lira instigated it, one-armed, against the last of Thornwater's autumn — and both were true at once, because both were.
   140	
   141	She did it at the house door at midday, to the steading's woman, in the voice she used for a distance: "It's his birthday. Nineteen. We've got three days' marks and a healer and a mule, and I want your common room and whatever you'll sell us that isn't bread." The woman looked at the sling's knot, said "Well," which was a ledger line, and named a price, and Lira paid it out of the pouch one-handed without putting the pouch down, which she had been practising for a week and had not said so.
