     1	# Chapter 15 — Strata
     2	
     3	Karis took the common room on the hundred and third day and did not give it back for a week.
     4	
     5	He catalogued the taking, because he had watched her take rooms before — a locked annex on a bluff, a long table by founding-era shelves, a bench above a gorge — and had never once seen it done to a room that had a hold's business in it. The steading's woman gave her the long table under the window. Karis gave the woman, in exchange, three days of the middle box's paper for the hold's own accounts, which the woman had been keeping on a board, and the woman looked at the paper and at Karis and said, "Well," and after that the common room was Karis's from first light until the fire was lit for supper, and the hold ate in the yard when the weather let it.
     6	
     7	The table, by the third morning. The rubbings first, because they were the largest: eleven sheets, one per upright, charcoal on the registry's own paper turned to its blank side, each sheet the height of the stone it had been taken from and cut to that height, so that the eleven lay on the table in the order the uprights stood and at their own sizes, tallest to shortest, a ring laid flat. The cord, coiled, the knots at the fives still in it. The notebook, open to the perimeter's page: *sixty-two cords and twenty strides; twelve hundred and fifty-six and a piece; ten cords in from the stone to the middle; a circle*. The stake's number, thirty-one, copied under the registry's mark drawn from memory. Seln's charcoal circle, copied off the carter's map onto its own sheet, with the three words under it in her small hand. The chips, in their row, each on its own square of cloth. And the three boxes, open, standing on the floor at her left hand in the order she reached for them, which he had learned in three days and could have reached for himself.
     8	
     9	The carvings she kept to herself for the first four days, and he let her. He had counted the bands on the second upright at the ring and stopped; she had copied all eleven and had been going over the sheets since with a pencil and a second sheet beside each, and the second sheets were filling with marks in rows, and the rows were a tally. He knew a tally when he saw one. He did not ask what she was counting until the fourth evening, and then she said, without looking up, "The kinds. They're in no alphabet I own, and I own more than the registry prints; I said so at the ring and I've had four days to be wrong about it and I'm not. Every band is the same kind of line and never the same line, and I want to know how many kinds there are before I say anything about any of them." He asked how many. "I have a number. I'm not saying it until I've a second site to check it against. A count from one place is a count. From two it's a fact." She went back to the pencil. It was the first time in four years he had heard her refuse to say a number, and he filed that as a measurement of the number.
    10	
    11	She worked with the voice she had. It had come back, as Oryn said it would, in pieces, over days — hoarse and usable by the birthday, spent on a quarter of an hour's argument about apples that night — and she budgeted it now the way she budgeted paper: a low thing that went on her when she pushed it and came back when she rested it, and she rested it by writing. She said the findings aloud, one at a time, as they came, to whoever was at the table, and wrote each one beneath the saying, and the writing was the entry and the saying was for them, which was a rule he had learned from a one-armed man at a plank and which she had arrived at on her own.
    12	
    13	He sat at the table's end with the log, most days, and did what he was for.
    14	
    15	---
    16	
    17	The rest of the unit found the week's shape and kept to it, and Thornwater went on round them, because a hold does not stop for a table.
    18	
    19	It was the back of the autumn. The carts came in from the lower fields with the last of the grain and went up the ladder to the loft in sacks, past the place where six people slept, so that by the week's end they slept on the hold's winter and not on its floor; the mornings had a skin of ice on the trough that the boy broke with a stick before the mule could drink; the woman's second chimney smoked from first light now and not from supper, and the common room, which was Karis's, was the warm room, and the hold's people found reasons to come through it and look at the ring laid flat on the table on their way to somewhere else. Nobody touched a sheet. The woman had said something at the house door on the first morning, and after that nobody touched a sheet.
    20	
    21	Brom moved boxes. He did it as he did everything that weighed something: without being asked, in the order Karis would want them, the mended hand doing its share and the string restriction three days expired, so that the crossbow went back on his belt where the hook was. Lira, who could not hold paper down with her left hand and would not be told she could not, learned to hold it with the right and a stone from the yard, and sat across from Karis for hours at a time with her arm across her chest reading the rubbings upside down, because she had never once in her life been near a thing she could not read without trying. Seln kept the door end, with the yard behind him and the case on his back, and said nothing for whole mornings, and once, when Karis reached for a sheet that was under his elbow, moved the elbow before she had finished reaching.
    22	
    23	Oryn worked.
    24	
