     1	# Chapter 43 — Three Facts
     2	
     3	The fourth finding was not about stone, and Karis gave it on the hundred and eighth morning in a different voice from the others.
     4	
     5	"This one is his," she said, and nodded toward the end of the trestle, where Seln stood. "I have it as he said it, at the stake, and I have gone over it with him twice since. It is the only leaf in that box which is about people. So I am entering it once, in four sentences, and I am not entering it again anywhere, in a margin or a table or a note."
     6	
     7	"Why once?" said Brom.
     8	
     9	"Because a thing that is written twice is a thing that somebody means to use," said Seln. "I have filed a great deal of paper. I know which leaves are drawn out of a box first."
    10	
    11	Karis had the leaf ready, and she read it standing, as a one-armed man read a line under an eave.
    12	
    13	"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
    14	
