    20	
    21	She altered it, in ink, and initialled the alteration.
    22	
    23	"And the maps?" said Lira. "He counted eight sheets with holes in them. That's not in your four."
    24	
    25	"It is not in the four because nobody in this barn has seen it but one person, once, as a boy, when he was counting corners. I have his memory entered where he gave it, on the leaf of the ninety-seventh, with *entered as said* beneath." Karis touched the roll of thin paper that had the ring of charcoal on it. "That ring lies on mine. It is the best thing that I have to hang a guess on, and I am not promoting a guess to a finding because I should like it to be one."
    26	
    27	Cael stood by the shutter with a slip of bark in his fingers and listened to four sentences.
    28	
    29	He had been ruling a line through that iron since a wash on the road east. There had been a query sent down to a registry, asking which way certain people had gone; and a paper under a broker's seal, with a sum written twice; and a stake. At a bar of iron eleven days ago he had seen for the first time which way along that line he was looking. He had expected to find the whole of it on her leaf. It was not there. There was a stamp, and a number, and a length of time, and a measure; and under those she had written, plainly, the three things that she did not know.
    30	
    31	It was a smaller leaf than the one in his head, and he saw that it was the better one, since everything on it would still be true if every guess of his turned out to be wrong.
    32	
