     5	Lira went up the ladder first, one-handed, because she had been going up ladders one-handed for two weeks and had stopped waiting to be watched. He went up after her. The walk was three planks wide with a rail on the yard side and nothing on the other but the top of the wall and the country past it, and he catalogued the country because he had not seen it from this height before: the void road going east out of the gate and over the first rise into the dark, toward a bend two days off that he could not see and could have drawn; the scrub going grey either side of it; the lower fields stripped to stubble, with the last cart's ruts still in them; a carter's fire a mile out, one point of orange, somebody walking the road quiet and camping short of the wall because the gate shut at dusk; the first star. She sat down with her back to the rail and her legs out along the planks and the arm across her chest, and looked at the road, and did not look at him, because she had said *later* on a road with her face turned away and this was later and she was giving him the same face to say it to.
