+## Tracked passages
+
+| chapter:line | Manuscript opening words | Source sentence followed | Kind | Beat chain to break without opening the source |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 38:13 | “It was the first thing that she had said…” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:11` — “It was the first thing she had said in twenty-one minutes…” | near-verbatim | Both sentences mark Oryn's first utterance after the long silence and immediately classify it by what kind of utterance it is: a weighted word in the source, a healer's question in the manuscript. Let the question stand without narrating that it is her first speech, or establish the silence after she speaks rather than before the professional gloss. |
+| 39:31 | “Then it's hers,” said Lira… | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:117` — “Seln said, ‘Good,’ in the voice for an inventory…” | near-verbatim | A character gives a short assent or ruling, and the narrator immediately declares that assent to be the whole discussion. Keep Lira's decision but let somebody act on it, change the subject, or leave a silence; do not close the exchange with “that was the whole…” machinery. |
+| 40:231 | “He read it aloud, and then he went through it again…” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:161` — “He read it twice, the second time for the count…” | varied | The protected Pike copy and required Gault recollection are excluded. The added handling keeps the source progression: Cael reads the copy, rereads it with special attention, isolates its decisive wording, recalls Gault's sentence, and contrasts the two men's uses of *instruments*. Have the carter or Seln verify Pike's count once, then let Gault's line surface later from a different trigger; alternatively begin on the Gault memory before Cael finishes reading the bark. |
+| 40:245 | “The door shut behind him. He had brought a keg…” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:163` — “There was nothing else in the carter's bag with his name on it…” | varied | Hesk's absent package is required; this staging is not. Both passages move from a delivery containing nothing else for Cael, to his involuntary check of the door or departure space, to companions noticing the check and answering it with food-handling instead of speech. Discover the missing package while packing the satchel later, or let Cael ask the carter directly and receive a plain route answer before the birthday resumes. |
+| 40:319 | “He did it that night, alone, as she had set it.” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:179` — “He did the thing she had asked him to do that night, alone…” | split | The first self-reading is required. Its entrance nevertheless keeps the source's full preparation order across several sentences: night and sleeping companions, darkness, Cael on his back, hands placed on his own body, imitation of Oryn's procedure, then the count and inward movement. Enter with the reading already under way and the first failed search for a bank, or begin from the physical surprise of his current returning to his wrist and supply the solitude only afterward. |
+| 41:245 | “She did not set the pen down, and she did not write.” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:133` — “She did not put the pen down. She held it over the page…” | near-verbatim | The hard stop and unwritten hypothesis are required; the sentence construction is not. Both keep the pen raised rather than set down, put its nib a finger above a clean page, prolong the suspension, and make non-writing the result. Start with the blank leaf being returned to the box, or have Karis cap the ink before the reader learns what she refused to record. |
+| 43:85 | “Cael had put no question to her in four days.” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:145` — “He sat at the table's end with his own book shut…” | varied | Both passages move from Karis's unwritten hypothesis to Cael's own withheld sentence, explicitly distinguish the two disciplines or sentences, then align them as two different refusals concerning the same underlying thing. Separate the comparison: let Karis finish and leave, then disclose Cael's parallel only at the gate-walk, or begin with Cael's debt and allow the reader—not Cael—to notice the resemblance later. |
+
+## Densely tracked scene
+
+### Chapter 40, scene 4 (lines 198–258)
+
+Re-enter on Gault's sentence already in Cael's mind, triggered by the word *instruments* as somebody else reads Pike's bark once. Let Seln or Oryn check the count after Cael's comparison rather than giving Cael the source-shaped first read / second read / recollection progression. Move Hesk's absence out of the doorway beat altogether: reveal it later when Cael opens the satchel to file the bark, or when the birthday table is cleared. That preserves the protected copy, Gault callback, westbound clerk's copy and missing package while breaking both inherited chains in the scene.
+
+No other scene needs wholesale rerouting; its single finding can be repaired locally from the table above.

